.TH "SERIALPORT.CONNECTED" "3JS" "May 19, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
SerialPort.connected \- SerialPort: connected property
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Secure context:\f[R] This feature is available only in secure
contexts (HTTPS).
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Dedicated Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]connected\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property of the
\f[CR]SerialPort\f[R] interface returns a boolean value that indicates
whether the port is logically connected to the device.
.SH DESCRIPTION
When a wireless device goes out of range of the host, any wireless
serial port opened by a web app automatically closes, even though it
stays logically connected.
In such cases, the web app could attempt to reopen the port using
\f[CR]SerialPort.open()\f[R].
.PP
However, if the wireless device was intentionally disconnected (for
example, if the user chose to disconnect it using the operating system
control panel), the web app should refrain from reopening the port to
prevent reconnecting to the wireless device.
.PP
The following snippet shows how the \f[CR]connected\f[R] property can be
used to distinguish between these two cases:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] ports = \f[B]await\f[R] navigator.serial.getPorts();
\f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] port \f[B]of\f[R] ports) {
  \f[B]if\f[R] (port.connected) {
    \f[I]// The port is logically connected\f[R]
    \f[I]// automatically try to reopen the port\f[R]
    \f[B]await\f[R] port.open({ baudRate: 9600 });
  } \f[B]else\f[R] {
    \f[I]// The port is not logically connected; at this point you could\f[R]
    \f[I]// prompt the user to make sure the Bluetooth device is available, and\f[R]
    \f[I]// Show a \(dqconnect\(dq button to allow them to try opening the port if desired\f[R]
  }
}
.EE
.SH VALUE
A boolean \(em \f[CR]true\f[R] if the port is logically connected, and
\f[CR]false\f[R] if not.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Logging when a port is connected
The following snippet invokes \f[CR]Serial.requestPort()\f[R] when the
user presses a \f[CR]<button>\f[R], prompting them to choose a serial
port to connect to, then logs a message to the console reporting the
connection status:
.IP
.EX
requestPortButton.addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, \f[B]async\f[R] () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  \f[B]const\f[R] port = \f[B]await\f[R] navigator.serial.requestPort();
  console.log(\(gaRequested serial port. Connected: ${port.connected}\(ga);
});
.EE
.SS Logging connection status on connect and disconnect
You can use the following snippet to log the connection status when the
\f[CR]connect\f[R] and \f[CR]disconnect\f[R] events fire:
.IP
.EX
navigator.serial.addEventListener(\(dqconnect\(dq, ({ target: port }) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(\(gaConnect event fired. Connected: ${port.connected}\(ga);
});

navigator.serial.addEventListener(\(dqdisconnect\(dq, ({ target: port }) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(\(gaDisconnect event fired. Connected: ${port.connected}\(ga);
});
.EE
